85:A path from $s$ to $t$ in a graph~$G$ is an {\it $s$-$t$-path} of $G$, and $s$ and $t$ are called its {\it terminals}.  Two pairs $(s_1,t_1)$ and $(s_2,t_2)$ are {\it disjoint} if $\{s_1,t_1\}\cap \{s_2,t_2\}=\emptyset$. In 1980, Shiloach~\cite{Sh80} gave a polynomial-time algorithm for testing if a graph with disjoint terminal pairs $(s_1,t_1)$ and $(s_2,t_2)$ has vertex-disjoint paths $P^1$ and $P^2$ such that each $P^i$ is an $s_i$-$t_i$ path. This problem can be generalized as follows.
