@@ -0,0 +1,198 @@
+# arXiv:2105.06349 problem generator
+
+| Profile | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate | 32-bit integer tuple at shipping |
+| Intuition | reduction recognition |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 3, Lemma 4 |
+
+This is an honest **licensed-reduction** family, not a claim that the solver is
+searching an arbitrary claw-free graph directly.  The solver receives the
+paper's rail-and-clause construction of a line graph, but the compact witness
+is a Boolean rail-choice vector and the useful reasoning is over GF(2).
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+The source is Kern, Martin, Paulusma, Smith, and van Leeuwen,
+[“Disjoint Paths and Connected Subgraphs for H-Free Graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06349).
+For each ternary XOR equation the generator makes its four equivalent 3-CNF
+clauses and applies the explicit reduction in Lemma 4: positive/negative
+variable rails and clause vertices form a base graph `B`; the instance is the
+line graph `L(B)`, with two prescribed terminal sets.  Because every line graph
+is claw-free, this lies in the paper's `K1,3`-free regime.
+
+The answer chooses one rail for each input wire.  Gate equations extend those
+bits to all wires.  The checker then constructs both vertex sets in `L(B)` and
+checks terminal containment, disjointness, and connectivity by exact endpoint
+incidence.  It never reads the planted answer.  Generation samples the input
+assignment first and carries it through the circuit and Lemma 4, so no generated
+instance is solved to obtain its certificate.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Track A would be false.  Theorem 1.1 says fixed-`k` Disjoint Paths is
+polynomial-time solvable even without an `H`-free restriction; Lemma 3 gives a
+polynomial algorithm for fixed-`k` Disjoint Connected Subgraphs when
+`H` is an induced subgraph of `sP1+P4`; Lemma 7 reduces the `P4`-free case to
+matching; and Section 6 gives `O(3^N k m)` dynamic programming in general.
+The hardness side relevant to the graph construction is Lemma 4: 2-Disjoint
+Connected Subgraphs is NP-complete on line graphs, via 3-SAT.
+
+This generated distribution has a still stronger reference algorithm: recover
+the XOR equations and use exact Gaussian elimination over GF(2).  At shipping
+size (`V=192`) the scalar implementation solved 8/8 instances in about 0.045 s,
+using 99,926 bit operations and 23,473 pivot tests on average.  The compact
+route notices that the CHECK rows use
+`I + T + T^(n/2)`, whose inverse for power-of-two `n` is
+`T^(n/2-2) + T^(n-2) + T^(n-1)`, then reverses the affine XOR gates.  That takes
+224 XORs.  The point is the gap between recognizing this structure and carrying
+out general elimination without a tool.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is the complete `demo` instance from `make_instance(n=4, gates=4, seed=2)`:
+
+```text
+Find a compressed witness for two disjoint connected subgraphs in a claw-free line graph.
+
+All bits below are in GF(2): XOR is addition modulo 2. Wire labels v0,
+..., v7 are identifiers, not an ordering. Every displayed
+equation has three distinct wires. Its four listed 3-bit codes are exactly
+the falsifying rows, in clause-slot order. A code abc creates the 3-CNF
+clause that is false at (a,b,c): use a positive literal for code bit 0
+and a negated literal for code bit 1.
+
+The graph is specified exactly as follows. Make a positive and a negative
+rail for every wire, in the displayed RAIL ORDER. On a rail, place that
+literal's clause occurrences in increasing (equation id, clause slot) order.
+Join consecutive vertices of each rail. Between consecutive wire gadgets
+join both rail ends to both next rail starts. Join the two starts of the
+first gadget by edge e and the two ends of the last gadget by edge f. For
+each clause make one clause vertex and join it to its three occurrence
+vertices. Call this base graph B. The problem graph is the line graph L(B):
+each edge of B is a vertex, and two such vertices are adjacent exactly when
+their B-edges share an endpoint. Thus L(B) is claw-free.
+
+Terminal set Z1 is {e,f}. Terminal set Z2 is every B-edge from a clause
+vertex to an occurrence vertex. A witness bit 0 chooses the positive rail
+and bit 1 the negative rail for S1; S2 uses the complementary rails and all
+Z2 vertices. The gate rows extend the input bits in step order. The CHECK
+rows must all hold. These choices then specify concrete vertex sets S1,S2
+in L(B); validity means they are disjoint, connected, and contain Z1,Z2.
+
+Number of input bits: 4
+Input wires in answer order:
+  v1 v0 v7 v4
+Rail order:
+  v2 v1 v0 v3 v6 v7 v4 v5
+
+GATE rows (semantic form z = a XOR b XOR rhs):
+  step 000, E1: v6 = v7 XOR v1 XOR 0; codes=111,100,001,010
+  step 001, E7: v2 = v7 XOR v4 XOR 0; codes=100,010,111,001
+  step 002, E0: v3 = v2 XOR v7 XOR 0; codes=100,111,001,010
+  step 003, E2: v5 = v3 XOR v0 XOR 1; codes=000,101,110,011
+
+CHECK rows (the operand order shown is part of the data):
+  E6: v6 XOR v5 XOR v3 = 1; codes=000,011,110,101
+  E3: v5 XOR v3 XOR v2 = 0; codes=111,001,010,100
+  E4: v3 XOR v2 XOR v6 = 0; codes=001,010,100,111
+  E5: v2 XOR v6 XOR v5 = 1; codes=000,101,110,011
+
+Return exactly 4 bits, one for each input wire in the listed
+answer order. Bits are 0-indexed positions; no separators, spaces, repeats,
+or ellipsis are allowed inside the bit string.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that bit string.
+Example: <answer>0101</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>0001</answer>`.  `verify(inst, [0,0,0,1])` returns
+`(True, "ok")`; flipping its first bit returns
+`(False, "parity check E4 fails: got 1, expected 0")`.  A person can solve this
+four-bit demo on paper by testing the CHECK equations and reversing four gates.
